{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794437407,"nonce":"su87f0pi/+B2Q5komRWvFEZhlkA=","message":"Committed as f496c52, not pushed.\n\n`hxn package` and the `hxn.Bind` macro.\n\nWhat `hxn.Bind` is was decided with the user on 2026-11-11. The scope document has it build the Haxe classes in memory from the manifest. It was built as a resolver instead: the classes stay the files hxn-gen writes, and the macro chooses among them. The reason is that the classes and the adapter they call come from one run of one emitter, so their names and signatures cannot differ; a macro that built classes would be a second emitter in another language, versioned apart from the adapter shipped beside it. The headers and the hxcpp glue are generated files a package has to carry anyway.\n\n- `hxn package [hl] [cpp] [--wasm] [--release] [--out \u003cdir\u003e]` builds each runtime named (both when none is) and gathers a directory: `\u003clibrary\u003e.hxn.json`, `hxn/` (hxn's Haxe classes), `binding/hl` and `binding/cpp` (the generated classes), `include/`, `native/\u003ctriple\u003e/` (`\u003clibrary\u003e.hdll`, `lib\u003clibrary\u003e_cpp.a` or `\u003clibrary\u003e_cpp.lib`, `\u003clibrary\u003e.wasm`), `haxelib.json` and `extraParams.hxml`. The default directory is `\u003ctarget\u003e/hxn/\u003clibrary\u003e/package`. Only `native/` depends on the machine, so a second run elsewhere or with `--wasm` adds to it.\n- `hxn.Bind.lib(\"\u003clibrary\u003e\")` is an init macro (`--macro`), not `@:build`: the class path has to be set before anything is typed. It finds the package through `Context.resolvePath(\"\u003clibrary\u003e.hxn.json\")`, checks the manifest's version and name, adds `binding/\u003chl|cpp\u003e`, and stops there under `display`. For hxcpp it defines `HXN_INCLUDE`, `\u003cLIBRARY\u003e_INCLUDE` and `\u003cLIBRARY\u003e_LIB`, and `HXCPP_M64` or `HXCPP_ARM64` on Windows and `HXCPP_M32` for Emscripten when none of the three is given. For HashLink it copies the library beside the `.hl` output after generation.\n- The triple is `-D hxn_triple`, else `wasm32-unknown-emscripten` under `emscripten`, else `wasm32-wasip1` or the Emscripten one under `wasm`, else the host's: `Sys.systemName()` with `uname -m`, or PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE on Windows; for hxcpp `HXCPP_ARM64` or `HXCPP_M64` name the machine if given.\n\nChecked: a defines set by the macro reach hxcpp's build XML; stock HashLink 1.15 on Linux has `.` in its runpath and Ash and macOS fall back to the working directory, so a library beside the program is found with no loader variable; `haxelib dev` on the package and `-lib greeter` work with a haxelib.json of name, version and description (tried in a repository made by `haxelib newrepo`).\n\ntests/cli/run.sh now packages the library it made and builds the program against the package alone, on each of its four targets.\n\nOpen:\n- haxelib.json has what `haxelib dev` needs. `haxelib submit` also wants a license from its own list, contributors and a release note, which the command does not write.\n- A package carries hxn's Haxe classes. Two packages made by different versions of hxn put two copies of `hxn.*` on the class path and the first wins.\n- No cross builds: `native/` gets the host's triple or a wasm one. Android and iOS, which the scope document lists, need `--target` on the command and their defines in the macro.\n- A Linux host whose Rust is musl, or a Windows one whose Rust is gnu, packages under a triple the macro does not guess; `-D hxn_triple` names it.\n- `-hl out.c` (HashLink/C) gets the classes and no library.\n- `disable_unicode_strings`, which hxcpp needs under Emscripten, is left to the program's build.\n\nRun: tests/cli/run.sh on Ash, hxcpp, Ash wasm and Emscripten on macOS, and on stock HashLink on the NUC. Not run: Windows and hxcpp on Linux, which CI does on push; Ash in its jit and interp modes for the package step.","files":null}]}